WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.890
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:08.540
This is a followup work to a technique we
have talked about earlier.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.030
We have seen how different creatures learned
to walk, and their movement patterns happened

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:16.230
to be robust to slight variations in the terrain.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.000
In this work, we imagine these creatures as
a collection of joints and links, typically

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.490
around 20 links.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:27.890
Depending on what actions we choose for these
individual body parts in time, we can construct

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.869
movements such as walking, or leaping forward.

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:36.550
However, this time, these creatures not only
learn to walk, but they also monitor their

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:41.789
surroundings and are also taught to cope with
immense difficulties that arise from larger

00:00:41.789 --> 00:00:43.539
terrain differences.

00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:48.889
This means that they learn both on character
features, like where the center of mass is

00:00:48.889 --> 00:00:54.210
and what the velocity of different body parts
are, and terrain features, such as, what the

00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:00.409
displacement of the slope we're walking up
on is or if there's a wall ahead of us.

00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:04.110
The used machinery to achieve this is deep
reinforcement learning.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:10.159
It is therefore a combination of a deep neural
network and a reinforcement learning algorithm.

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:15.789
The neural network learns the correspondence
between these states and output actions, and

00:01:15.789 --> 00:01:20.800
the reinforcement learner tries to guess which
action will lead to a positive reward, which

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.310
is typically measured as our progress on how
far we got through the level.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.390
In this footage we can witness how a simple
learning algorithm built from these two puzzle

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:35.920
pieces can teach these creatures to modify
their center of mass and adapt their movement

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:42.070
to overcome more sophisticated obstacles,
and, other kinds of advertisites.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:48.170
And please note that the technique still supports
a variety of different creature setups.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:52.920
One important limitation of this technique
is that it is restricted to 2D.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.340
This means that the characters can walk around
not in a 3D world, but on a plane.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:03.649
A question whether we're shackled by the 2D-ness
of the technique or if the results can be

00:02:03.649 --> 00:02:06.389
applied to 3D remains to be seen.

00:02:06.389 --> 00:02:12.200
I'd like to note that candidly discussing
limitations is immensely important in research,

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:17.280
and the most important thing is often not
we can do at this moment, but the long-term

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.489
potential of the technique, which, I think
this work has in abundance.

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:26.430
It's very clear that in this research area,
enormous leaps are made year by year, and

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.540
there's lots to be excited about.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:33.360
As more papers are published on this locomotion
problem, the authors also discuss that it

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:39.069
would be great to have a unified physics system
and some error metrics so that we can measure

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:42.250
these techniques against each other on equal
footings.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:47.630
I feel that such a work would provide fertile
grounds for more exploration in this area,

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:51.540
and if I see more papers akin to this one,
I'll be a happy man.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.489
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

